maya: Hey folks — trying to get the JS SDK working in a fresh Expo + Hermes app and running into multiple issues, posting notes here in case anyone's seen them.

maya: Symptoms: runtime crash on import with 'unexpected token export' (ESM/CJS mismatch), console errors about missing globalThis.crypto.getRandomValues, and fetch streaming hangs (ReadableStream not behaving). Typescript defs also complain about 'AbortSignal' on RN.

maya: Minimal repro: expo init, add @redwood-inference/sdk (local tarball), then `import { createClient } from '@redwood-inference/sdk'` in App.tsx -> Metro throws on startup: `SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'`.

chet: That usually means the package ships ESM only and Metro isn't resolving it. Are you using expo prebuilt metro config? Metro still has trouble with packages that only expose .mjs.

maya: Yep. I tried patching metro.config.js to include `resolver: { sourceExts: ['js','jsx','ts','tsx','mjs'] }` and blacklist node_modules transformIgnorePatterns but still hit the export token for some dependencies inside the SDK build.

benji: Two quick workarounds we use internally: 1) ship a CJS build for rn target (dist/cjs) and point `react-native` field in package.json to it, or 2) publish an ESM+ReactNative-specific entry that is transpiled to ES5. Both fix metro import.

maya: For the crypto piece — Hermes doesn't implement web crypto. `globalThis.crypto` is undefined, so the SDK's `crypto.getRandomValues` call fails deep in keygen. Tried `expo-random` but the SDK expects getRandomValues sync.

sam: We did this in a sample app: quick polyfill adapter —

sam: ```js
// polyfills/crypto.js
import * as Random from 'expo-random';
if (!globalThis.crypto) {
  globalThis.crypto = {
    getRandomValues(arr) {
      const bytes = Random.getRandomBytes(arr.length);
      for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) arr[i] = bytes[i];
      return arr;
    }
  }
}
```

sam: Put that at the top of App.tsx before any SDK imports and it unblocks key generation. Not ideal but works for onboarding/sample apps.

olivia: Streaming note: RN Fetch polyfills don't always expose native ReadableStream. Our workaround is to replace streaming codepath with a chunked polling fallback when running in RN. Can detect `typeof ReadableStream === 'undefined'` and use incremental fetch with Range headers or server-sent chunks on our side.

maya: Typescript complaints: `Property 'signal' does not exist on type 'RequestInit'` and `AbortSignal` types missing. Metro TS config in expo uses lib dom false by default.

benji: For typings we built a small `rn-augment.d.ts` shipped in the SDK when `engines.platform === 'react-native'` that declares the missing dom bits we rely on (AbortSignal, ReadableStream minimal interfaces). It’s a tiny patch: `declare global { interface AbortSignal { aborted: boolean } }` etc.

maya: OK — plan of action: 1) add RN-targeted CJS build that metro can consume, 2) include a tiny runtime check + helpful error message when global crypto missing with a suggested polyfill snippet, 3) ship `rn-augment.d.ts` and update README with expo quickstart.

chet: Agreed. Also add CI step in devex repo to run a minimal expo snack/e2e smoke test on publish so we don't regress.

olivia: Can I take README + sample app? I can add an `expo/` example that imports the polyfill and runs a quick token call.

maya: Yes please. Benji — can you add the CJS build and type augmentation in the next release candidate?

benji: Will do. I'll also add a runtime check at SDK init: if (!globalThis.crypto || !globalThis.crypto.getRandomValues) console.warn("Expo/Hermes detected: crypto.getRandomValues missing — see docs for polyfill");

devex-bot: Note saved: issue/DEVEX-349 created for RN ESM/CJS + polyfill + examples. Assignee: benji.

